      "key": "manual_walkthrough_law",
      "statement": "Automation must remain true to manual traversal. Using only declared surfaces, a human should identify current typed asset/state; next lawful vector (constitutional transition contract) and public function (its constructive program); required inputs/evaluators/output/environment; construct or assess next asset/checkpoint; and repeat to target-relative closure. Automation may accelerate, safeguard or repeat this process and render prompts, scaffold, select steps, run workers, collect evidence and assess closure within declared authority. It may not invent another process, skip undeclared nodes, fabricate authority, collapse transitions opaquely without declared refinement, require unavailable implementation-carrier knowledge, or merge installed-builder and developing-product authority during self-hosting. Inability to explain manual traversal means automation is not yet method-safe.",
